+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: msgpack-rust (`rmpv` + `rmp-serde`)
+
+`hegeltest` 0.28.2 was added as a dev-dependency to `rmpv` and `rmp-serde`
+(via `cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`; hegeltest's MSRV is
+1.86, the crates pin `rust-version = "1.85"` — see Skill feedback).
+15 hegel property tests plus one deterministic `#[ignore]`d bug reproducer
+were added to the existing test files.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### rmpv — `rmpv/tests/mirror.rs`
+
+All draw from a recursive `Value` generator covering integers at every
+MessagePack width boundary (`u64`/`i64` full range plus explicit boundary
+sampling), f32/f64 built from raw bit patterns (NaN payloads, infinities,
+subnormals), full-Unicode strings, binary, ext (all FixExt sizes reachable),
+and nested arrays/maps up to 3 container levels.
+
+1. **`mirror_arbitrary_value`** — round-trip: `read_value` inverts
+   `write_value` for every `Value`, floats compared bitwise so NaN
+   round-trips are asserted too. Evidence: this is the crate's core promise;
+   it generalizes the existing quickcheck `mirror_*` tests (which excused NaN
+   mismatches instead of checking bits and only covered scalars).
+2. **`mirror_value_ref_agrees_with_value`** — consistency: the zero-copy
+   decoder (`read_value_ref(..).to_owned()`) agrees with the owned decoder on
+   every valid encoding. Evidence: `ValueRef::to_owned` docs state the two
+   representations are equivalent; both decoders implement the same format.
+3. **`integer_encoding_uses_smallest_width`** — spec conformance with an
+   independent oracle: the expected (marker byte, encoded length) is
+   recomputed in the test from the MessagePack spec's smallest-width rule and
+   compared against `write_value` output. Evidence: `rmp::encode::write_sint`
+   docs: "the serializer SHOULD use the format which represents the data in
+   the smallest number of bytes".
+4. **`length_markers_use_smallest_width`** — same spec oracle for
+   str/bin/array/map length headers (fixstr/str8/str16/str32, bin8/16/32,
+   fixarray/array16/array32, fixmap/map16/map32), with lengths targeted at
+   the 15/16, 31/32, 255/256, 65535/65536 boundaries.
+5. **`truncated_encoding_fails_to_decode`** — validation/rejection: every
+   strict prefix of a valid encoding is incomplete, so both decoders must
+   return an error. Evidence: MessagePack is self-delimiting; decoding
+   consumes exactly the bytes written.
+6. **`mutated_encoding_never_panics_and_decoders_agree`** — robustness:
+   corrupting one byte of a valid encoding never panics either decoder, and
+   both decoders still agree on Ok/Err and the decoded value.
+
+### rmpv — `rmpv/tests/decode.rs`
+
+7. **`arbitrary_bytes_never_panic_and_decoders_agree`** — parse robustness on
+   completely arbitrary bytes; also asserts `read_value` and `read_value_ref`
+   agree. Evidence: decoders return `Result` and are documented to error, not
+   panic, on bad input; `PREALLOC_MAX` in `decode/value.rs` shows the crate
+   explicitly hardens against malicious length claims (issue #151).
+8. **`nesting_depth_limit_is_enforced_without_crashing`** — the recursion
+   budget of `read_value_with_max_depth` / `read_value_ref_with_max_depth` is
+   enforced exactly (each array level costs two budget units), exceeding it
+   yields `Error::DepthLimitExceeded`, and both decoders agree. Evidence:
+   `rmpv::decode::MAX_DEPTH` docs ("The maximum recursion depth before
+   Error::DepthLimitExceeded is returned"). The generated nesting depth is
+   capped at 250 to protect the test process from the real bug below.
+   - **`known_bug_stack_overflow_within_max_depth`** (`#[test] #[ignore]`) —
+     deterministic pinned reproducer for the stack overflow described under
+     Suspected bugs. Ignored because the failure aborts the whole test
+     process (SIGABRT), which would take the suite down with it.
+
+### rmp-serde — `rmp-serde/tests/round.rs`
+
+9. **`struct_roundtrip_under_any_config`** — generate-the-configuration
+   round-trip: a rich struct/enum tree (all four enum variant shapes, nested
+   struct, tuple, map, `Option`, `serde_bytes::ByteBuf`, `char`, `IpAddr`
+   whose serde impl is human-readable-sensitive) round-trips under every
+   serializer configuration: default (struct-as-tuple) vs `with_struct_map()`
+   crossed with `with_human_readable()` (deserializer flag matched, as serde
+   requires). Evidence: `config.rs` documents these wrappers; existing unit
+   tests cover single fixed configs only.
+10. **`integer_roundtrip_all_widths`** — `to_vec`/`from_slice` round-trip for
+    i8–i128 and u8–u128 at full range with 64-bit boundary boosting; i128/u128
+    exercise the dedicated 16-byte-bin encoding path (`read_i128_marker`).
+11. **`float_roundtrip_preserves_bits`** — f32/f64 from raw bit patterns
+    round-trip bitwise (NaN payloads, infinities, subnormals).
+12. **`std_types_roundtrip`** — String (full Unicode), char (all planes),
+    `Option`, `Vec<u64>`, `BTreeMap<i64, String>`, tuples,
+    `serde_bytes::ByteBuf`.
+13. **`encoding_matches_rmpv_value_encoding`** — cross-crate consistency:
+    for data with a direct `rmpv::Value` equivalent (ints, floats, bool,
+    str, bytes, sequences, maps, `Option`), `rmp_serde::to_vec` produces
+    byte-for-byte the same encoding as `rmpv::encode::write_value`.
+    Evidence: both crates encode via `rmp`'s `write_sint`/`write_uint`/
+    `write_str`/... smallest-width functions.
+14. **`truncated_struct_encoding_fails`** — every strict prefix of a valid
+    `to_vec` encoding fails `from_slice`.
+
+### rmp-serde — `rmp-serde/tests/decode.rs`
+
+15. **`from_slice_never_panics_on_arbitrary_bytes`** — parse robustness of
+    `from_slice` across ten target types (ints, floats, String, Vec, map,
+    Option, ByteBuf, tuple, derived struct/enum).
+
+## Test results
+
+- Full workspace `cargo test`: **all green** (21 test binaries/doc-test runs,
+  0 failures). The only ignored tests are three pre-existing `#[ignore]`s and
+  the new process-aborting bug reproducer.
+- **Liveness check**: temporarily inverted assertions in
+  `mirror_arbitrary_value` (hegel shrank the counterexample to `Nil`) and in
+  `struct_roundtrip_under_any_config` (shrank to the all-zero/empty `Outer`
+  with `named = false, human = false`), then reverted. Both suites really
+  execute and shrink.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all 15 hegel tests were temporarily raised to
+  `test_cases = 1000` and passed; committed tests use the default (100).
+- The depth property failed with a process abort on its first run (stack
+  overflow, SIGABRT) — investigated below; after pinning the bug the
+  committed suite is deterministic and green.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### `rmpv`: stack overflow in debug builds well within its own `MAX_DEPTH` guard
+
+`rmpv::decode::MAX_DEPTH = 1024` exists precisely to stop unbounded-recursion
+crashes on malicious input, and in release builds it works as designed:
+probing nested single-element arrays gives `Ok` up to 512 levels (each level
+costs 2 budget units), `Err(DepthLimitExceeded)` from 513, and no crash even
+at 4000 levels.
+
+In **debug builds**, however, `read_value` overflows the test thread's stack
+at roughly **370–380 nesting levels** — less than half the depth the guard
+permits (bisected: depth 360 decodes, depth 380 aborts with
+`thread ... has overflowed its stack` / SIGABRT). `read_value_ref` is
+similarly affected. So any application that decodes untrusted MessagePack
+with rmpv and runs a debug binary (or any thread with a comparable stack
+budget) can be crash-DoSed by a ~200-byte input despite the guard.
+
+- Reproducer: `rmpv/tests/decode.rs::known_bug_stack_overflow_within_max_depth`
+  (`cargo test -p rmpv --test decode -- --ignored`, debug profile).
+- Found by: `nesting_depth_limit_is_enforced_without_crashing` before its
+  generator was capped (the cap comment references the reproducer).
+- Likely fix direction: `MAX_DEPTH` is calibrated too high for real frame
+  sizes (debug-build `read_value_inner` + `read_array_data` frames are ~5–10
+  KiB per level); compare serde_json's default recursion limit of 128.
+
+No other property failed: integer/length markers match the spec oracle
+exactly, the two rmpv decoders never disagreed on ~100k arbitrary/mutated
+inputs, and all rmp-serde round-trips (including 128-bit integers, NaN bit
+patterns, and all four serializer configs) held.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`Value::String` with invalid UTF-8 round-trips** — unsound by design:
+  `rmpv/src/encode/value.rs` deliberately encodes invalid-UTF-8 strings with
+  the *bin* marker, so they decode as `Value::Binary`. The `Value::String`
+  docs explicitly allow deserializers latitude here. The round-trip generator
+  therefore only produces valid-UTF-8 strings (documented in a comment);
+  invalid-UTF-8 payloads still reach the decoders via the mutation and
+  arbitrary-bytes properties.
+- **Direct deep-input assertion against `MAX_DEPTH`** — the first version of
+  the depth property fed up to 4000 real nesting levels through `read_value`
+  and asserted Ok/Err against `MAX_DEPTH`. That is exactly what crashed: the
+  property was sound but untestable in-process in debug builds. Replaced by
+  the `read_value_with_max_depth`-based property (budget ≤ 600, nesting ≤
+  250) plus the ignored reproducer.
+- **Round-trip under mismatched human-readable flags** — never asserted:
+  serde's contract requires serializer and deserializer to agree on
+  `is_human_readable`; types like `IpAddr` legitimately use different wire
+  shapes per flag. Configurations are generated as matched pairs.
+- **Round-trip under `with_bytes(BytesMode::ForceIterables/ForceAll)`** —
+  never asserted: `config.rs` documents these modes "may break some
+  implementations of `Deserialize`", i.e. round-tripping is explicitly not
+  promised.
+- **NaN equality via `PartialEq`** — the pre-existing quickcheck mirror tests
+  excuse NaN mismatches (`eq || xs.is_nan()`); rather than inheriting that
+  weakened property, the hegel tests compare floats bitwise. (Kept the
+  quickcheck tests in place per the skill's supplement-don't-delete default.)
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference's Setup section was spot-on twice: the MSRV conflict
+  (`cargo add` failed against `rust-version = "1.85"`, fixed with
+  `--ignore-rust-version` exactly as documented — worth flagging to
+  maintainers if these tests are upstreamed) and the `.hegel/` failure-DB
+  cleanup after deliberate liveness failures.
+- The `compose!`-returning-`BoxedGenerator` pattern for recursive generators
+  worked first try, including the documented `'static` lifetime spelling and
+  no-`move` rule. Without that reference note, the `E0283` from a recursive
+  `#[hegel::composite]` would have cost real time.
+- The main skill's "process-aborting bugs" guidance was directly load-bearing
+  here: the depth property's first failure was a SIGABRT with no hegel
+  report, and the keep-an-`#[ignore]`d-reproducer + cap-the-sibling-generator
+  recipe is exactly what the final suite does.
+- Friction: there is no global case-count override, so the exploratory 10x
+  run meant sed-editing 15 attributes and reverting; an env var like
+  `HEGEL_TEST_CASES` would make that one command.
+- Friction (minor): `one_of!` over branches of different generator types did
+  require `.boxed()` on every branch (the reference's "only if the compiler
+  demands unification" — it always demanded it for
+  `integers` + `sampled_from` mixes).
+- Counterexample output that prints draws as `let val = ...;` statements made
+  the liveness checks and the shrink quality (`Nil`, all-zero `Outer`) easy
+  to verify at a glance.
